---
import Icon from "../Icon.astro";
import Term from "../Term.astro";
import Try from "../Try.astro";
import "../sternzeit/sternzeit.css";
import Rays from "./Rays.astro";
import "./nachtgestirn.css";
import Resolutions from "../Resolutions.astro";
import LunationSlider from "../sternzeit/LunationSlider.astro";
import { STRIP_RESOLUTIONS } from "./resolution-options";

/** The disc with a close-up marked, and the close-up as the color map alone, with the relief, with its shadows, and displaced. */
interface Props {
    id?: string;
}
const { id = "surface" } = Astro.props;
---

<figure class="tiles-figure wide" id={id} data-surface>
    <div class="tiles-panel as-seen split" style="--count: 5">
        <canvas aria-label="The Moon, and a close-up of its terminator drawn from the color map alone, with the relief, with the relief's shadows, and with the relief displaced, rendered with WebGPU"></canvas>
        <span class="tiles-start">
            <Resolutions options={STRIP_RESOLUTIONS} chosen="large" />
            <span class="view-buttons"><button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="albedo" aria-pressed="true" title="NASA's color map of the Moon; off, a uniform surface of the Moon's albedo, so the relief and its shadows show alone">albedo</button></span>
            <span class="tiles-hint footnote">Rendered with WebGPU</span>
        </span>
        <svg class="tiles-overlay" data-field="marker" aria-hidden="true"></svg>
        <span class="tiles-ends">
            <span class="view-buttons"><Rays /></span>
            <span class="view-buttons">
                <button type="button" class="view-icon" data-field="download" title="Download the disc and its close-ups as PNG" aria-label="Download as PNG"><Icon name="download" /></button>
            </span>
        </span>
    </div>
    <ul class="tiles-labels" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr)">
        <li><span class="tiles-name">the disc</span> <span class="tiles-value">metered</span></li>
        <li><span class="tiles-name">map</span> <span class="tiles-value">a smooth sphere</span></li>
        <li><span class="tiles-name">relief</span> <span class="tiles-value">the slopes shaded</span></li>
        <li><span class="tiles-name">shadows</span> <span class="tiles-value">cast through the heights</span></li>
        <li><span class="tiles-name">displaced</span> <span class="tiles-value">rays marched through the heights</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tiles-panel tycho-panel" data-export="tycho-slice">
        <span class="view-buttons">
            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="sliceShadows" aria-pressed="true" title="The shadows: each point's march towards the Sun and the share of the Sun's disc it sees; off, every point sees all of it">shadows</button>
            <button type="button" class="view-toggle" data-field="sliceNormals" aria-pressed="true" title="The normals: each point lit by the cosine of its normal to the Sun; off, by the share of the Sun it sees alone">normals</button>
        </span>
        <svg class="tycho-slice" data-field="slice" role="img" aria-label="A slice through the crater Tycho from west to east, from LOLA's heights: the surface lit as much as it sees of the Sun, its normals, and one point's march towards the Sun" title="Drag along the slice for another point's march; double click for the tip of the shadow"></svg>
        <span class="tycho-names">
            <span class="tiles-hint footnote" data-export-text>west</span>
            <span class="tiles-hint footnote" data-field="sliceName" data-export-text></span>
            <span class="tiles-hint footnote" data-export-text>east</span>
        </span>
    </div>
    <p class="view-status tycho-note" data-field="sliceNote"></p>
    <LunationSlider />
    <p class="view-status" data-field="note"></p>
    <figcaption class="figure-caption">
        The relief along the <Term of="Terminator">terminator</Term>, from the 4K maps: the whole disc at the chosen
        moment, metered, with the close-up marked on it, and the close-up four times, a stop more open for the low
        Sun. The color map alone on a smooth sphere, where the terminator is a soft ramp and every crater a flat stain;
        with the relief, the slopes turned to the Sun bright and away from it dark; and with the shadows marched through
        the heights, the crater floors filling with shadow and the peaks beyond the terminator catching the light; and displaced, each
        ray marched through the heights, so the near rims hide the floors behind them and the peaks stand up. The
        close-up keeps to the terminator at Tycho's 43° south as the slider runs from new moon to new moon, and so
        passes over the crater at sunrise and at sunset. Under it, how the
        pass gets there, on a slice through the crater Tycho from west to east, from the heights of the 8K strip, a texel
        a kilometer apart, the heights four times as tall and the Moon's curve kept: each texel's normal from its two
        neighbors' heights, and how much of the Sun's disc it sees, marched towards the Sun from three quarters of a
        texel on, each step a quarter farther than the last and read from as coarse a level of the map as the steps are
        apart, so no peak falls between them. The highest angle the terrain reaches is its horizon; the share of the
        Sun's half degree above it lights the point, all of it, some or none. The surface is drawn thick and bright as
        far as it sees the Sun, its normals as long as they turn to it and bright where they see it, the shadows dark
        under the last rays grazing the terrain, and one point's march drawn out, with the map as the march reads it
        from there dashed beside the real one, coarser with the distance, the tip of the shadow nearest the middle unless you drag to another. Tycho and the slice are marked on the disc.
        <Try label="Sunrise over Tycho" set="date=2026-11-18T06:00Z" fig={id}>
            in the slice, the eastern rim's shadow across the floor, the central peak's top in the light.
        </Try>
        <Try label="Sunset over Tycho" set="date=2026-12-02T06:00Z" fig={id}>
            the light from the west, the western rim's shadow reaching for the peak.
        </Try>
    </figcaption>
</figure>

<script>
    import { surfaceFigure } from "./surface";

    for (const root of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("figure[data-surface]")) surfaceFigure(root);
</script>
